# ============================================================================
# SIR — fresh (jshttp/fresh)
# ============================================================================

UNIT fresh
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
# Pure: no clock, no RNG, no I/O, no network, no env. Behavior is a total
# function of the two header objects passed in. The ONLY ambient dependency is
# the host JS engine's `Date.parse`, which is itself a pure function of its
# string argument (it parses; it does NOT read the wall clock). So there is NO
# SEAM to inject: the unit is deterministic above and below `Date.parse`. To
# keep independent re-emitters convergent, the generator restricts date inputs
# to GMT-anchored RFC 1123 / RFC 850 / ISO-8601 forms and clearly-unparseable
# strings (see SCOPE > OUT-OF-ENVELOPE re: locale-ambiguous date strings).
#
# READ-ONLY-SIDE-EFFECT addendum: N/A. The unit performs no reads of any
# ambient source and no writes; it only reads its own two object arguments.

SIG  fresh(reqHeaders: object, resHeaders: object) -> boolean
     # reqHeaders, resHeaders are plain objects mapping lowercased HTTP header
     # names to their string values. The unit only ever consults these keys:
     #   reqHeaders['if-modified-since']
     #   reqHeaders['if-none-match']
     #   reqHeaders['cache-control']
     #   resHeaders['etag']
     #   resHeaders['last-modified']
     # All other keys are ignored. Header VALUES are treated as strings
     # (string operations: ===, regexp .test, .charCodeAt, .substring, '+').

DEPENDS-ON  none
     # Internal helpers parseHttpDate and parseTokenList are defined inline and
     # described fully below; they are NOT external dependencies.

EXPORT-SHAPE  default
     # In the source this is CommonJS `module.exports = fresh`. The unit IS the
     # whole public surface of the module. A blind re-emitter targeting an ES
     # module MUST do:  export default fresh
     # i.e. the function itself is the default export (no named wrapper, no
     # object). It is invoked as a 2-argument function returning a boolean.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline — encode EXACTLY; order and short-circuits matter)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Let:
#   modifiedSince = reqHeaders['if-modified-since']
#   noneMatch     = reqHeaders['if-none-match']
#
# NODE 1 — Unconditional request (early return false)
#   If BOTH modifiedSince and noneMatch are FALSY (undefined / missing / null /
#   '' empty string / 0 / false), return false immediately.
#   "Falsy" is JS truthiness: the empty string '' counts as ABSENT here.
#   This is the only place the two fields are tested for mere presence.
#
# NODE 2 — Cache-Control: no-cache (early return false)
#   cacheControl = reqHeaders['cache-control']
#   If cacheControl is TRUTHY AND it matches the no-cache directive regexp,
#   return false (force stale, end-to-end reload semantics).
#   The regexp (EXACT, anchored to comma/string boundaries, lazy whitespace):
#       /(?:^|,)\s*?no-cache\s*?(?:,|$)/
#   Semantics of this regexp:
#     - "no-cache" must be a standalone directive: preceded by start-of-string
#       or a comma, and followed by a comma or end-of-string.
#     - Between the boundary and the token, and between the token and the next
#       boundary, only ASCII whitespace (\s) is allowed (lazy, zero-or-more).
#     - It is CASE-SENSITIVE and must be lowercase "no-cache".
#     - It does NOT match substrings like "no-cacheX" or "xno-cache" (those
#       lack a directive boundary). "no-store" does not match.
#     - It DOES match: "no-cache" ; "public, no-cache" ; "no-cache, public" ;
#       " no-cache " (leading/trailing whitespace ok via \s*?) ;
#       "max-age=0, no-cache".
#     - It does NOT match "max-age=0" alone, "no-store" alone, "private".
#   NOTE: \s includes space AND tab AND newline AND other Unicode whitespace
#   that JS \s matches. Use the regexp verbatim; do not paraphrase it.
#
# NODE 3 — If-None-Match (ETag) check
#   This node runs ONLY when (noneMatch is truthy AND noneMatch !== '*').
#   - If noneMatch === '*' : the ETag check is SKIPPED ENTIRELY (it is treated
#     as a wildcard that matches any/no etag — the resHeaders.etag is NOT even
#     inspected). Proceed to NODE 4.
#   - If noneMatch is falsy (only possible when modifiedSince is truthy and we
#     passed NODE 1): the ETag check is SKIPPED. Proceed to NODE 4.
#   Otherwise (truthy and not '*') perform matching:
#     etag = resHeaders['etag']
#     a) If etag is FALSY (missing / '' / undefined), return false (stale).
#     b) etagStale = true
#        matches = parseTokenList(noneMatch)   # see TOKENIZER below
#        For each token `match` in `matches`, in order:
#          if ( match === etag
#               OR match === 'W/' + etag         # token is the weak form of etag
#               OR 'W/' + match === etag ) {     # etag is the weak form of token
#             etagStale = false; BREAK
#          }
#        i.e. a token matches the etag if ANY of three EXACT-STRING equalities
#        holds. This is NOT a normalize-then-compare: there is NO general "strip
#        W/ from both sides". The ONLY weak-prefix accommodation is the literal
#        string-prepend 'W/' on exactly one side. Consequences (encode these):
#          - token '"foo"'    , etag '"foo"'    -> match (case 1)
#          - token '"foo"'    , etag 'W/"foo"'  -> match (case 3: 'W/'+token === etag)
#          - token 'W/"foo"'  , etag '"foo"'    -> match (case 2: token === 'W/'+etag)
#          - token 'W/"foo"'  , etag 'W/"foo"'  -> match (case 1, identical)
#          - token 'W/"foo"'  , etag 'W/"bar"'  -> NO match
#          - token 'WW/"foo"' , etag 'W/"foo"'  -> NO match (only a single 'W/' is considered)
#          - token '"foo"'    , etag 'WW/"foo"' -> NO match
#        ETag comparison is fully CASE-SENSITIVE and byte-exact on the quotes,
#        the W/ prefix casing (must be uppercase 'W', slash), and contents.
#     c) After the loop, if etagStale is still true, return false (stale).
#        Otherwise fall through to NODE 4.
#
# NODE 4 — If-Modified-Since (date) check
#   This node runs ONLY when modifiedSince is truthy.
#   lastModified = resHeaders['last-modified']
#   modifiedStale =  (lastModified is FALSY)
#                 OR NOT ( parseHttpDate(lastModified) <= parseHttpDate(modifiedSince) )
#   If modifiedStale is true, return false (stale).
#   Else fall through.
#   Freshness rule: the response is fresh w.r.t. dates iff a last-modified value
#   is present AND its parsed timestamp is <= the parsed if-modified-since
#   timestamp (resource not changed since the client's copy). EQUAL timestamps
#   are FRESH (<= is inclusive). If EITHER date fails to parse, parseHttpDate
#   yields NaN, and any comparison with NaN is false, so NOT(false)=true =>
#   modifiedStale=true => stale (return false). Missing last-modified => stale.
#
# NODE 5 — Default
#   If none of the above returned false, return true.
#
# COMBINATION / PRECEDENCE RULE (CRITICAL):
#   The three checks (no-cache, if-none-match, if-modified-since) are combined
#   as a LOGICAL AND via sequential short-circuit returns: ANY failing check
#   returns false; true is returned only if every applicable check passes.
#   Specifically when BOTH if-none-match and if-modified-since are present:
#     result = (etag check passes) AND (date check passes).
#     - etag matches but date is stale  -> false
#     - etag mismatches                 -> false (date check never reached)
#     - etag matches AND date fresh     -> true
#     - if-none-match === '*' AND date fresh -> true (etag node skipped, date governs)
#     - if-none-match === '*' AND date stale -> false
#   When only ONE conditional header is present, that single check governs
#   (the absent check's node is skipped). no-cache (NODE 2) overrides BOTH
#   (returns false before either check, even if the etag would have matched).
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HELPER — parseHttpDate(date) -> number
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   timestamp = date && Date.parse(date)
#   return (typeof timestamp === 'number') ? timestamp : NaN
#   Notes:
#     - Uses the host engine's Date.parse. For unparseable strings Date.parse
#       returns NaN, whose typeof is 'number', so parseHttpDate returns NaN
#       (the ternary's NaN branch is effectively unreachable in stock V8; the
#       `date && ...` guard means a falsy `date` short-circuits to the falsy
#       value, but NODE 4 only calls this with truthy strings).
#     - It does NOT read the current time. Identical input string -> identical
#       output under a fixed engine. Treat Date.parse as a pure collaborator
#       supplied by the host runtime (same engine on re-emitter and oracle).
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HELPER — parseTokenList(str) -> string[]   (the If-None-Match list splitter)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Splits a header value into comma-separated tokens, trimming ONLY leading
#   ASCII spaces (0x20) and dropping trailing spaces before a comma/end. It is
#   a single forward scan with two indices start,end over str (i = 0..len-1):
#     - char 0x20 (SPACE): if (start === end) { start = end = i+1 }   # leading-space skip
#       (a space is "leading" only while no non-space char has been seen since
#        the last comma; once a non-space char advanced `end`, a space does NOT
#        advance `end`, so trailing spaces are excluded from the token, but
#        an INTERNAL space followed by more non-space chars IS retained because
#        the later non-space char advances `end` past the space.)
#     - char 0x2c (COMMA ','): push str.substring(start,end); start = end = i+1
#     - any other char: end = i+1
#   After the loop: push str.substring(start,end)   # final token (always pushed)
#   ONLY the space character 0x20 is special — TAB (0x09) and other whitespace
#   are ordinary chars and are NOT trimmed. Worked examples (verbatim outputs):
#     '"foo"'           -> ['"foo"']
#     '"foo" '          -> ['"foo"']          # trailing space dropped
#     '  "foo"'         -> ['"foo"']          # leading spaces dropped
#     '"a", "b", "foo"' -> ['"a"','"b"','"foo"']
#     '"a",   "foo"'    -> ['"a"','"foo"']    # multiple post-comma spaces dropped
#     '"a" "b"'         -> ['"a" "b"']        # internal space RETAINED (single token)
#     ' '               -> ['']               # one empty token
#     ','               -> ['','']            # two empty tokens
#     '"foo",'          -> ['"foo"','']       # trailing empty token
#     '\t"foo"'         -> ['\t"foo"']        # tab kept verbatim (not trimmed)
#   The empty/whitespace tokens generally fail to match a real quoted etag, so
#   they produce a stale (false) result unless the etag itself is that string.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EDGE CASES (all VERIFIED against fresh@0.5.2)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   fresh({}, {})                                              -> false   (NODE 1)
#   fresh({'cache-control':'no-cache'}, {'etag':'"x"'})        -> false   (NODE 1: no conditional headers; cache-control alone is NOT a conditional header, so the unconditional early-return fires first)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'*'}, {'etag':'"x"'})               -> true
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'*'}, {})                           -> true    (etag not inspected for '*')
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'*','cache-control':'no-cache'}, {'etag':'"x"'}) -> false (no-cache wins)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"'}, {'etag':'"foo"'})         -> true
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"'}, {'etag':'"bar"'})         -> false
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"'}, {})                       -> false   (no etag)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"'}, {'etag':''})              -> false   (empty etag falsy)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"'}, {'etag':'W/"foo"'})       -> true    (weak res)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'W/"foo"'}, {'etag':'"foo"'})       -> true    (weak req)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'W/"foo"'}, {'etag':'W/"foo"'})     -> true
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'W/"foo"'}, {'etag':'W/"bar"'})     -> false
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"a", "b", "foo"'}, {'etag':'"foo"'}) -> true
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"a", "b"'}, {'etag':'"foo"'})      -> false
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':D}, {'last-modified':D})        -> true    (equal, <=)
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':D}, {'last-modified':OLDER})    -> true
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':D}, {'last-modified':NEWER})    -> false
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':D}, {})                         -> false   (no last-modified)
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':'garbage'}, {'last-modified':D}) -> false  (req date NaN)
#   fresh({'if-modified-since':D}, {'last-modified':'garbage'}) -> false  (res date NaN)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"','if-modified-since':D}, {'etag':'"foo"','last-modified':OLDER}) -> true   (both pass)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"foo"','if-modified-since':D}, {'etag':'"foo"','last-modified':NEWER}) -> false  (date stale)
#   fresh({'if-none-match':'"bar"','if-modified-since':D}, {'etag':'"foo"','last-modified':OLDER}) -> false  (etag mismatch short-circuits)
#   where D='Mon, 01 Jan 2018 00:00:00 GMT', OLDER earlier, NEWER later.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Output is a boolean. The harness records {result:true|false} (or {__throw}
# if a non-codec value triggered a throw — not reachable from in-envelope
# inputs, which are objects of string/number values; property access on plain
# objects never throws). No expecteds are hand-authored.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire NODE 1..5 pipeline, the
#   no-cache regexp /(?:^|,)\s*?no-cache\s*?(?:,|$)/ VERBATIM, the three-way
#   exact-string ETag matching (incl. the 'W/'-prepend asymmetry), the
#   parseTokenList space-only/leading-trim tokenizer, and the inclusive (<=)
#   NaN-aware date comparison via parseHttpDate. All small constants only; NO
#   carried-data table. This unit has NO carried data.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT (side APIs / stateful mutators): none. Pure read-only.
#
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented; NOT emitted by genInputs — a blind re-emitter
# still reconstructs them from the rules above, but they are not graded because
# they are non-portable or non-codec):
#   - Header VALUES that are not strings: e.g. numbers. The source uses string
#     ops; a numeric `if-none-match` that is not '*' would call
#     parseTokenList(number).length -> str.length is undefined on a number,
#     `.charCodeAt` absent -> the scan would behave unexpectedly. Keep header
#     values as STRINGS. (The codec round-trips numbers, but behavior is
#     ill-defined / version-fragile, so out of envelope.)
#   - Function-valued or Symbol-valued headers: not codec-faithful; excluded.
#   - LOCALE/TIMEZONE-AMBIGUOUS date strings (e.g. asctime 'Mon Jan  1
#     00:00:00 2018', or bare 'YYYY-MM-DD' without zone): Date.parse may
#     interpret these in LOCAL time, which differs across engines/hosts and
#     could make re-emitters diverge on the boundary. genInputs emits ONLY
#     GMT/UTC-anchored RFC-1123, RFC-850, and ISO-8601-with-Z forms (and
#     clearly-unparseable 'garbage' strings), all of which Date.parse maps
#     deterministically and engine-independently.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROVENANCE  fresh@0.5.2
            repo: jshttp/fresh (https://github.com/jshttp/fresh)
            license: MIT
            source file: index.js (entry per package.json "main" default; the
              package ships only index.js as its code file)
